# Side-by-Side Comparison: Original vs Improved

## Endpoint: GET /users

### Original (Vulnerable)
```javascript
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

**Issues:**
- ❌ No input validation
- ❌ No error handling
- ❌ No pagination support
- ❌ Generic response format
- ❌ Vulnerable to abuse

### Improved (Secure)
```javascript
app.get('/users',
  [
    body('limit').optional().isInt({ min: 1, max: 100 }).toInt(),
    body('offset').optional().isInt({ min: 0 }).toInt(),
  ],
  handleValidationErrors,
  (req, res) => {
    try {
      const limit = Math.min(req.query.limit || 10, 100);
      const offset = req.query.offset || 0;

      if (offset < 0 || limit < 1) {
        return res.status(400).json({
          success: false,
          error: 'Invalid pagination parameters'
        });
      }

      const paginatedUsers = USERS_DB.slice(offset, offset + limit);

      res.json({
        success: true,
        data: paginatedUsers,
        pagination: {
          limit,
          offset,
          total: USERS_DB.length,
          hasMore: offset + limit < USERS_DB.length
        }
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching users:', error);
      res.status(500).json({
        success: false,
        error: 'Failed to fetch users',
        ...(NODE_ENV === 'development' && { details: error.message })
      });
    }
  }
);
```

**Improvements:**
- ✅ Input validation on query params
- ✅ Error handling with try-catch
- ✅ Pagination support
- ✅ Consistent response format
- ✅ Protected against abuse
- ✅ Detailed logging
- ✅ Environment-aware error messages

