## Issue #2: No Input Validation

### ❌ BEFORE (Vulnerable)
```javascript
app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  // No validation!
  // What if someone sends: { name: 123, email: "not-an-email" }?
  // What if payload is 100MB?
  // What if they try NoSQL injection?
  const user = req.body;
  res.json(user);
});
```

**Attack Examples:**
```javascript
// NoSQL injection attempt
{ name: { "$ne": null }, email: "x" }

// XSS attempt  
{ name: "<script>alert('xss')</script>", email: "x@x.com" }

// Type confusion
{ name: 123, email: true }

// Missing required fields
{ name: "Alice" } // email missing
```

### ✅ AFTER (Secure)
```javascript
app.post('/api/users',
  // Validate and sanitize
  body('name')
    .trim()
    .isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 })
    .withMessage('Name must be 1-100 characters')
    .escape(), // Sanitize HTML
  body('email')
    .isEmail()
    .withMessage('Invalid email format')
    .normalizeEmail(), // Normalize email

  (req, res) => {
    try {
      // Check validation results
      const errors = validationResult(req);
      if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
        return res.status(400).json({
          error: 'Validation failed',
          details: errors.array(),
        });
      }

      // At this point, data is safe!
      const user = {
        name: req.body.name,
        email: req.body.email,
      };

      res.status(201).json(user);
    } catch (error) {
      handleError(error, res);
    }
  }
);
```

**What it prevents:**
- ✅ NoSQL injection
- ✅ XSS attacks
- ✅ Type confusion
- ✅ Buffer overflows
- ✅ Missing required fields
- ✅ Invalid formats

